WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.966
Sigma Reporting and Performance Benchmarking

2
00:00:04.966 --> 00:00:09.366
Welcome back to our session on quality baseline.

3
00:00:09.366 --> 00:00:12.232
as we come down the backstretch on this topic here.

4
00:00:12.233 --> 00:00:18.366
Let's imagine that we have out in front of us some business.

5
00:00:18.366 --> 00:00:25.199
And in that business, we have a number of key processes that we've identified.

6
00:00:25.200 --> 00:00:30.100
We’ll call that process one

7
00:00:30.100 --> 00:00:37.366
through process N. Where N is some big number. So, from 1 to 2, to 3, to 4, to 5 to N.

8
00:00:37.366 --> 00:00:42.432
Where N might be a thousand or N might be, you know, 500, or whatever.

9
00:00:42.433 --> 00:00:47.799
But that's the critical processes that drives that business.

10
00:00:47.800 --> 00:00:54.800
These are the processes that create this business. That defines this business.

11
00:00:54.800 --> 00:01:01.800
I think we can understand that. Now, each process provides output.

12
00:01:01.800 --> 00:01:08.266
Has some output. I’ll just call output.

13
00:01:08.266 --> 00:01:14.666
And of course every output, in turn, has some number of things

14
00:01:14.666 --> 00:01:20.366
that can go wrong with it. Defect type A, type B,

15
00:01:20.366 --> 00:01:26.899
type C, type D. Same thing here type A. Maybe which is a different kinda A. Type B,

16
00:01:26.900 --> 00:01:31.966
different kind. C and D. Has a host of things that can go wrong with it.

17
00:01:31.966 --> 00:01:36.066
Now, what we can do is take this first process for example.

18
00:01:36.066 --> 00:01:39.332
And let's imagine it's a commercial process.

19
00:01:39.333 --> 00:01:45.366
Let's say for example it’s the hiring process of a corporation

20
00:01:45.366 --> 00:01:51.332
or could be a legal process of some type. Maybe filing for a patent.

21
00:01:51.333 --> 00:01:58.333
It could be a drilling process if it was industrial. Or maybe a wave soldering process.

22
00:01:58.333 --> 00:02:02.833
And it has output with different things that can go wrong.

23
00:02:02.833 --> 00:02:09.166
Well what we do, is we go out and we count, for some number of output.

24
00:02:09.166 --> 00:02:12.799
We count the things that can go wrong.

25
00:02:12.800 --> 00:02:18.033
And from that, we determined the yield of the process.

26
00:02:18.033 --> 00:02:22.833
On the flipside of that is the defects per million opportunities.

27
00:02:22.833 --> 00:02:29.066
Now, once I know the defects per million opportunities of that process.

28
00:02:29.066 --> 00:02:33.999
Let's say for example that it was around 65,000

29
00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:37.566
defects per million opportunities.

30
00:02:37.566 --> 00:02:40.732
That's roughly in this region right here.

31
00:02:40.733 --> 00:02:45.599
So, what I’m going to do, is I’m going to put a little dot right there to represent that process.

32
00:02:45.600 --> 00:02:52.566
Then I go do it again for another process. And this time maybe I find 30,000 defects.

33
00:02:52.566 --> 00:02:56.866
And I do that again for another process.

34
00:02:56.866 --> 00:03:02.232
And maybe I find 50,000 defects per million opportunities.

35
00:03:02.233 --> 00:03:07.533
I make a dot. And I start doing that for all

36
00:03:07.533 --> 00:03:10.799
of my critical processes in that business.

37
00:03:10.800 --> 00:03:15.133
Be they commercial or industrial. And I find them

38
00:03:15.133 --> 00:03:18.999
and they all start coming in right about this region.

39
00:03:19.000 --> 00:03:24.733
So, what I do. Very simply.

40
00:03:24.733 --> 00:03:28.733
These are all my baselines, now. That's a keyword there. That's all my baselines.

41
00:03:28.733 --> 00:03:33.266
That's the baseline of that process. Maybe the patent filing process.

42
00:03:33.266 --> 00:03:37.799
That’s the baseline of the customer documentation process for their needs.

43
00:03:37.800 --> 00:03:42.400
Any number of processes. But the key is this. Look here.

44
00:03:42.400 --> 00:03:46.300
You see them all centered around there? They’re all coming in around

45
00:03:46.300 --> 00:03:51.500
3 or 4 Sigma, with the average at about 3.5 Sigma.

46
00:03:51.500 --> 00:03:54.533
That's how we go about benchmarking.

47
00:03:54.533 --> 00:03:59.933
So, I establish my baseline for all these different processes.

48
00:03:59.933 --> 00:04:04.399
Then I just kinda look at them on a chart like this, where they all cluster.

49
00:04:04.400 --> 00:04:11.233
And I get the baseline of the baseline. Which is around somewhere in here, about 3.5 Sigma.

50
00:04:11.233 --> 00:04:17.966
So, now I can say I got about a 3 1/2 Sigma business.

51
00:04:17.966 --> 00:04:21.466
Now, if I compare that business to say my competitor,

52
00:04:21.466 --> 00:04:24.466
I get some data on them, I’m now benchmarking.

53
00:04:24.466 --> 00:04:24.499
And that's what we will talk about next, the power benchmarking

54
00:04:24.500 --> 00:04:29.500
Thank you for choosing SSMI.
